\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0444cc3a-ca2c-58ed-a888-9c05ac28591e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263038} {:answer/id #uuid "0b09f021-320e-59aa-9f9f-75ce8ccb4a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263106} {:answer/id #uuid "72ae0c79-f0db-50fc-96f1-3edd0b57e9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263994} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c39ec2-4cea-5168-adb4-8475364f8dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264725} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf2d475-5a1a-580f-9317-5a6ee7663626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263038} :answer-field/id #uuid "79ee8586-1fa9-562a-adbf-d61fb9fe20d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "436bb998-0141-522d-ba99-0fedc798b98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130852" :question/problem "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-15){R}_{2}$ to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A.$ Find the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." :question/worked-solution "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-15){R}_{2}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-15){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029edf87-92f0-5f4d-857c-fbcd96d1ad44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263022} {:answer/id #uuid "04c58135-ba05-51f9-b009-72ffcc1bed2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263044} {:answer/id #uuid "92720e0d-2494-5493-a17f-d03095b968db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264255} {:answer/id #uuid "a462712d-421d-50ca-982e-193a070b7aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264401} {:answer/id #uuid "e44c943e-a381-50e6-92bb-55c088607ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263044} :answer-field/id #uuid "d138b62f-6f1b-544f-a0dd-55662c209a48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a1edd9d-9d16-504c-8084-c1f6f0fde2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130853" :question/problem "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=18{R}_{1}$ to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A.$ Find the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." :question/worked-solution "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=18{R}_{1}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=18{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e039ee-8a6b-51ec-86b7-6d980d5d65d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263503} {:answer/id #uuid "4a438d53-d237-5702-b279-a9c54664081d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263639} {:answer/id #uuid "58ea5ffb-35fe-577b-8a88-4648837805e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263765} {:answer/id #uuid "bf73a4ac-fd00-53c8-acd2-c58c47a01158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264667} {:answer/id #uuid "d5062c22-d816-5246-b604-14b063340f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263639} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a2e5db5-7fe0-5bfd-95da-7abed420088a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fa0220a1-28ff-5de6-b75f-1ed62d2ea351" :question/math-academy-id "q-130859" :question/problem "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=6{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=6{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row  operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}:=6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f389b7a-bb36-5074-8c96-fb1ecf9fa6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263288} {:answer/id #uuid "8241a5d7-02a1-547d-baef-267aa901fd37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264119} {:answer/id #uuid "84a7fac4-e47e-5983-a206-08e59164fb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264139} {:answer/id #uuid "b1263628-59c7-546e-a4af-aac116f01421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264525} {:answer/id #uuid "c5653eec-b4a3-5077-bd82-07173a4abb55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264731}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264525} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd48ee5b-7dbe-500d-a87b-ee2f033ad6d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "837dfba2-f78f-5c76-b27f-bb2d2c133624" :question/math-academy-id "q-130860" :question/problem "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}.$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200326c6-4e9b-5f9d-8ea8-3c7fd8caa616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263292} {:answer/id #uuid "783d2411-8439-515d-972b-2e98645d0bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264043} {:answer/id #uuid "94702e89-66e6-5edd-9c50-630d134703e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264271} {:answer/id #uuid "96a95a1d-d7ee-55e3-9d5d-b9c01aab924a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264289} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a3316a-6a18-572b-8fbd-42d9ba63f793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264271} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f26578-678b-584b-94d3-3bf45adddf76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a5d6a7d0-f153-5a48-8ab8-2274611f50fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130862" :question/problem "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the three given elementary row operations. \n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "754569dc-c33c-5246-bef6-de39612bd634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264019} {:answer/id #uuid "853968c1-8cfe-5637-b142-164d9645f0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 15 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264148} {:answer/id #uuid "a34b9756-88e8-5714-ae4f-fa4f3d173d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264384} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5075fc-0c0f-5424-9ff7-b11495204153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264489} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a32961-3630-510e-b3e8-27971f2836e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264384} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cad95cc-57da-561f-b114-9f218f13d2b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "23bb6d19-f0bc-58f3-805e-627e1903136e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131256" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{34}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{34}=3$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1547a157-5d1c-579d-8e28-43f01384ad6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z-49 \\\\ z+7 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263197} {:answer/id #uuid "4e788748-8ecf-5384-9dc9-b10f1901c4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z+49 \\\\ z-7 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263680} {:answer/id #uuid "5112ff0b-e183-5f2a-870d-868bab6bfe11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z-7 \\\\ z+7 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263703} {:answer/id #uuid "e8be0e12-20ab-5f6f-879f-3c9f3d101ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z+7 \\\\ z-7 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265024} {:answer/id #uuid "fab00b53-cdf8-5de2-a356-0fb5c2c3aabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z+49 \\\\ 7-z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263680} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eec1d4b-7cf6-5f45-b740-2396d18b839a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b800a38-395b-5c94-96d7-0ac6c5b8d8a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131484" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 7 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+7y-8z &=0 \\\\ 0x+y-z &=-7 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+7y-8z &=0 \\\\ y-z &=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-z=-7\\,⇒\\,y=z-7\n$$\n\nand from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+7(z-7)-8z=0\\,⇒\\,x=z+49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=z+49 \\\\ y &=z-7 \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}z+49 \\\\ z-7 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a24b9ff-3299-52ca-952c-c1be9bd058bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263520} {:answer/id #uuid "52bcc4a6-4aee-5408-b40e-3a113af74682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263714} {:answer/id #uuid "7aef2613-2ab9-5ec5-bfd6-71a88fb27ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264065} {:answer/id #uuid "aae34e89-5ce2-5fab-8b6b-4924db39f5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264464} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a17cfc-dbc9-5d50-aa7d-45f75f51bd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264065} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a81f6ed-60f8-5e2d-84c7-99af7ff211c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b396d2f-e5d7-5e11-bc90-72d02b4ec262" :question/math-academy-id "q-131488" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z &=0 \\\\ 0x+0y-2z &=4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z &=0 \\\\ -2z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nStarting with the second equation $z=-2,$ we substitute into the first equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3(-2) &=0\\,⇒\\,x=2y+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2y+6 \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6 \\\\ y \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
